## Getting started<a id="getting-started"></a>

The modules run on the managed host, as ordinary Ansible modules do. Tell them
once per play how to call the product's command, with `module_defaults`:

```yaml
---
- name: {{ ('Configure ' ~ product | default('Example Product')) | to_json(ensure_ascii=False) }}
  hosts: "product_hosts"
  gather_facts: false
  become: true
  become_user: "product"
  module_defaults:
    group/{{ namespace }}.{{ collection_name }}.api:
      credential: "{% raw %}{{ product_credential }}{% endraw %}"
      exec:
        kind: "custom"
        executable: "/usr/bin/python3"
        argv:
          - "/opt/fixture/fixture_command.py"
        chdir: "/var/lib/fixture/owned-state"
        user: "1000"
  tasks:
    - name: "Set the quota"
      {{ namespace }}.{{ collection_name }}.fixture:
        app: "alpha"
        key: "quota"
        expected_id: "090d190b-c80a-4917-8532-6b62a2836d0c"
        limit: 50
      register: "__quota"
```

`exec` names the program to run: an absolute interpreter path, the absolute
path of the program, its working directory and the numeric UID the play runs
as. `app` and `key` select the object. `expected_id` is the identity you expect
it to have; the module refuses to write when it finds another object there.

The first run changes the limit and reports `changed: true`. A second run finds
it as declared and reports `changed: false`. In both cases the result holds the
object as the product stores it:

```yaml
__quota:
  changed: true
  changed_fields:
    - "limit"
  verified: "observed"
  fixture:
    app: "alpha"
    key: "quota"
    id: "090d190b-c80a-4917-8532-6b62a2836d0c"
    enabled: true
    limit: 50
    label: ""
```


