### Preview a change<a id="usage-fixture-plan"></a>

`fixture_plan` takes the options of `fixture`, reads the current state and
reports in `would_change` whether the declaration differs from it. It never
writes and always reports `changed: false`, so you can use it in a normal run,
for example to stop a play before a change you did not expect:

```yaml
- name: "See what the next task would do"
  {{ namespace }}.{{ collection_name }}.fixture_plan:
    app: "alpha"
    key: "quota"
    expected_id: "090d190b-c80a-4917-8532-6b62a2836d0c"
    limit: 50
  register: "__plan"
```

Modules: `fixture_plan`.


## How the modules behave<a id="module-behavior"></a>

All modules of the collection follow the same rules. The table gives each rule
in one line; the sections below add the details you need when a run surprises
you.

|       Topic       | Rule |
| ----------------- | ---- |
| Managed options   | A module changes only the options you set. |
| Finding an object | `app` and `key` select it; `expected_id` must match what is there. |
| Missing objects   | A module fails when the object does not exist. It does not create it. |
| Running a command | One foreground command at a time, in a clean environment, under one deadline. |
| Check mode        | Check mode reads and predicts; it never writes. |
| Failures          | A failed task says whether it changed anything. |
| Lost replies      | A module never sends a write twice. |
| Product versions  | An untested product series warns; a known-bad build fails before writing. |

### What a module manages<a id="behavior-ownership"></a>

A module changes only the options you set in the task. An option you leave out,
or set to `null`, keeps whatever value the product has. `false`, `0` and the
empty string are values: the module sets them.

Other objects and settings of the product stay untouched, also those of the
same `app`.

