### How a module finds an object<a id="behavior-identity"></a>

`app` and `key` together select one object. `expected_id` is the identity you
expect this object to have. When the product reports another identity, someone
has replaced the object since you wrote the task, and the module refuses to
write.

The object must exist. `fixture` fails when it is missing, in normal runs and
in check mode. `fixture_info` returns `fixture: null` when the product reports
that the object does not exist. A read that fails or returns incomplete output
never counts as "does not exist"; the task fails instead.

### How a module runs the command<a id="behavior-execution"></a>

A module runs the product's program as a foreground command and waits for it.
All commands of one task share one time budget, `deadline`, which is 5 seconds
unless you set it: the reads and identity checks, the write and the read after
it. When the budget runs out, the module ends the command and its child
processes.

The command gets an empty environment and a fixed locale, so variables of the
Ansible session do not reach the product. The credential travels on standard
input, not on the command line. Before a write the module checks that it runs
under the UID you named in `exec.user` and that the program does as well.

The `custom` kind of `exec` accepts one absolute program path under an absolute
Python interpreter. It does not enter containers and does not wrap the command
in `sudo` or `su`.

