### Check mode, plans and diff mode<a id="behavior-check-diff"></a>

In check mode (`--check`) a module reads the current state and predicts the
result, but sends no write. The `verified` value in the result tells you what
the reported state is based on:

- `observed`: the module read this state from the product. Nothing would
  change, or the module ran outside check mode and read the object back.
- `predicted`: the state shown is what the module expects after a write that
  check mode skipped, or that `fixture_plan` describes.

`fixture_info` always reports observed state.

With `--diff`, `fixture` shows the options that change, before and after,
without secrets.

### What a failed task reports<a id="behavior-failures"></a>

A failed task carries these values next to `msg`, so that a play or a person
can decide what to do:

|             Value              | Meaning |
| ------------------------------ | ------- |
| `error_category`, `error_code` | What kind of failure it was, as fixed words |
| `operation`                    | The step that failed |
| `app`, `key`                   | The object the task addressed |
| `changed`, `outcome`           | Whether the product's state changed: `unchanged`, `changed` or `unknown` |

The failure also carries `http_status`, which is always `null` here because
the modules send no HTTP request.

A module sends a write once. When the command fails, times out or returns
nothing usable, the module reads the object again to find out what happened:

- The read shows part of the change: `outcome: changed`.
- The read is not possible: `outcome: unknown`. Check the object before you run
  the task again.

A command that ran into the deadline was ended from outside, so it may have
done part of its work.

Results and error messages contain neither the credential nor the raw output
of the command.

### Product versions<a id="behavior-versions"></a>

A module reads the product version before a write or a prediction. On a
product series this release was not tested with, the module warns once and
continues. An operation known to fail on a particular product build fails
before it writes. [Compatibility](#compatibility) lists the tested series.

